{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: one application layout, discovered by convention, with a scaffold. Write notes/\u003cdate\u003e-application-layout-and-scaffold.md.\n\nBackground. Josh Rosen's article \"Architecting the 'No Skills' Application\" (X article 2112313639742935040, 2026-10-20) argues that an agent copies the first similar implementation it finds, so a repository should have one way to do each thing, let the build discover new modules from their location, and create standard structures with a generator that is itself tested. Hugh read it on 2026-10-20, agreed that dap is already well structured for most of it, and asked for design notes on the gaps. This note is the first gap. The planner filed it from that conversation.\n\nWhat the tree shows today (main 248aab8f, 2026-10-20).\n- Five application-like directories have four shapes. apps/counter: dap.application.json, definition.json (the first definition, counting, with no runtime user), commitments.json, pin.ts and commitments-pin.ts, and a tsconfig listed by hand in the root typecheck script. apps/jam: its own package.json, wrangler.jsonc and deploy directory, outside the workspaces. apps/room: the host Worker, with lanes and page as workspaces, and wrangler.lending.jsonc for the lending example. examples/lending: dap.application.json, definition.json, scripts/pin.mjs, a browser directory, and its Worker config over in apps/room. examples/grow: two manifests (check/dap.application.json and demo/application.dap.json), its own wrangler.jsonc, and no pin script.\n- The root registers each one by hand. package.json \"typecheck\" lists six tsconfig paths. package.json \"test\" lists eight node test files and one browser test. vitest.config.ts has three special-case project entries for counter, lending and grow. scripts/active-source.test.mjs has a regex that marks apps/counter and examples/ inactive. homepage/build.mjs and scripts/deploy-check-evaluate.mjs name applications.\n- docs/write-an-application.md is nine steps of prose: item types, acts, invariants, validate, pin, scenarios, create a scope, device command, publish a file. The pin step is a per-application script, copied twice.\n- Three sales packages (sales applications note, slices C1 to C4) and the market packages (brokered market note, M slices) are about to be written. Each author, person or agent, will copy whichever shape it finds first and will miss one of the root lists.\n\nWhat exists to build on. The self-contained applications note (main 40e37d85) fixes the manifest and the install model: presentation id separate from semantic id, install per room, distribution as a Git release by URI. The builder-room note (main 165d5a86) gives validatePackage and dap check (P1 is filed), and its section 6 starts an agent from a template or an installed package. The gate (scripts/gate.sh) already runs structural checks of our own (shared helpers, active source). docs/mcp-tools.json and docs/lanes-reference.md are the existing pattern for a generated file that a test refuses when it drifts.\n\nThe note decides.\n1. The one layout of an application directory: the manifest's path and the fields that discovery needs; where definitions, scenarios, pins, views, the Worker config and tests sit; what is optional.\n2. Discovery: the root typecheck, test, vitest projects, deploy check and release plan find applications by walking for the manifest. Every hand list above goes away or is generated and tested.\n3. Scope: whether jam and the room host are applications under this layout or hosts beside it, and what examples/ means afterwards. Recommendation to weigh: applications are definition packages; the host is one thing; the examples are applications and move or are renamed.\n4. The scaffold: a command (dap new application \u003cname\u003e, or similar) that writes a template application which validates, pins, carries one scenario and passes dap check. Pin becomes a command, not a per-application script.\n5. The generator's own test in the gate: generate into a scratch directory, then run typecheck, validate, pin, the scenario and the structural checks. This keeps the template from drifting from the checks, which is the one point of the article that dap does not yet have.\n6. What remains of docs/write-an-application.md: only what the generator cannot decide.\n7. Slices, sized, with the migration of the four existing shapes as one of them.\n\nAcceptance. The note lists every root file that names an application today and the convention that replaces each; the manifest fields that discovery needs; the generator's test; the open question for Hugh (item 3) with a recommendation; the slices. Document only. Checker review before landing. The note names no product other than GitHub and cites the article by author and title. Implementation requests follow after it lands.\n\nPriority. Hugh agreed on 2026-10-20 with this order: this note and the history note (filed beside it, which he called definitely important) first; the guard grammar note third.","body":{"conditions":"Document only; checker review before landing; no product names other than GitHub in the note; implementation requests follow after it lands.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}